    "created_at": "2020-09-18T11:15:53Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-09-18T11:15:53Z",
    "body": "I'll retract \"most\" as that's not backed up with evidence, just personally dealing with a lot of developer confusion around `SameSite` as a concept at all 😅\r\n\r\nRather, it feels confusing that these yield different results:\r\n\r\n```js\r\ncookieStore.set('opted_out', '1');\r\ndocument.cookie = 'opted_out=1';\r\n```\r\n\r\nHowever, after playing around a bit I'm realising that `Lax` is primarily beneficial if you want the cookie sent in the **header** on navigation, client-side `SameSite=Strict` cookies are actually available within the page on navigation. So, maybe the impact on apps that are primarily using cookies within JavaScript is minimal, `Strict` does seem like a good default there. It's only instances where a developer is setting a cookie from JavaScript that they would want sent to their server on initial navigation.\r\n\r\nI do like the idea of encouraging developers to set explicit defaults, so I'm not overly attached to this, but at the minimum I think there should be a clear \"Differences from `document.cookie`\" or \"Migration guide\" section.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
